  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nHigh pressure will dominate the forecast through the coming week, \nremaining centered over the Desert Southwest. This will keep \nconditions high and dry, with only a slight chance for an isolated \nshower or thunderstorm over the Southern Divide each afternoon. \nAbove normal temperatures will stick around through the period as \nwell, with near-record high temperatures each afternoon. Extreme \nHeat Warnings and Heat Advisories are in effect through at least \nTuesday evening. However, with little change in the pattern, these \nhighlights will likely be extended day-by-day through the end of the \nweek.\n\nA trough will transit across the Northern Rockies during the first \nhalf of the week, with a few weak shortwaves dipping south into \neastern Utah and western Colorado. There's not much moisture with \nany of these waves, but they will lead to some increased surface \nwinds, with marginal gusts to around 25 mph. With dry surface levels \nand hot temperatures in place, wherever fuels are still critical \nthere will be a chance for localized Critical Fire Weather \nConditions.\n\nLooking further out, toward the end of the workweek and the coming \nweekend, ensemble and deterministic guidance seem inclined to bring \na trough into the West Coast. This would finally displace the high \nto the east, potentially allowing moisture to be drawn up into the \nFour Corners once more. This will be something to watch over the \ncoming days as forecast confidence increases.",
